```{r setup, include = FALSE}
source("precompute-setup.R")
unlink(file.path(docs_root, "cohorts-demo"), recursive = TRUE)
```

A cohort decides which patients, admissions or ICU stays enter the study. In
rendehR the decision comes first: the selection runs in DuckDB over the raw
files, and only the selected rows, together with the context they need, are
written to Parquet or DuckDB. Nothing is imported before the cohort is known.

```{r project}
ehr_init_project(
  "mimic_iv_demo", base_dir = "cohorts-demo",
  raw_data_root = normalizePath("rendehr-demo/data/source_data")
)
```

```{r config, include = FALSE}
cfg <- jsonlite::read_json("cohorts-demo/config/ehr_config.json")
cfg$database$db_name <- "rendehr_demo"
jsonlite::write_json(cfg, "cohorts-demo/config/ehr_config.json",
                     auto_unbox = TRUE, pretty = TRUE, null = "null")
ehr_use_config("cohorts-demo/config/ehr_config.json")
```

The examples below use the MIMIC-IV demo downloaded in
`vignette("rendehR")`; `raw_data_root` points at that copy, so nothing is
downloaded twice.

## Where the cohort can come from

Any cohort request is one of six routes, and at most one may be given:

| You have | Pass |
|:--|:--|
| A list of identifiers | `direct_ids_path` |
| SQL that returns identifiers | `cohort_sql` (plus optional `setup_sql`) |
| An R function that returns identifiers | `cohort_fn` |
| A table of ICD codes | `icd_filter_path` |
| A definition bundled with the package | `cohort` |
| The whole source | `no_filter = TRUE` |

With none of them, the project's `cohort.profile` and `cohort.icd_filter_path`
decide. The profile defaults to `"generic"`; under `"scid"`, comorbidities and
injury severity run unless switched off.

```{r example-cohorts}
list_example_cohorts()[, c("name", "kind", "description")]
```

`scid` is an ICD-code cohort; `sepsis3` runs the packaged Sepsis-3 SQL. The
SQL definitions and their variants are described in
`vignette("sepsis-cohort-definitions")`.

## Look before you import

`select_cohort_ids()` runs the selection and returns a plan without writing
anything. Printing the plan reports the route, the key, the scope and the
counts; identifiers never appear on screen.

```{r plans}
scid <- select_cohort_ids("mimic_iv_demo", cohort = "scid")
sepsis <- select_cohort_ids("mimic_iv_demo", cohort = "sepsis3")
no_filter <- select_cohort_ids("mimic_iv_demo", no_filter = TRUE)
scid
```

The plan records `selection_key` and `selection_grain` (what you supplied),
`evidence_key` and `evidence_grain` (what the definition matched), the requested
and effective scope, and `requested_count`, `matched_count` and
`unmatched_count`. The normalised identifiers are in `plan$values`, for code
that needs them, not for printing.

## Identifiers, SQL, and ICD lists

A direct identifier file may be CSV, CSV.GZ, Parquet or RDS. Identifiers are
read as text, trimmed, deduplicated and matched against the source hierarchy.
A selection fails when the file is empty, malformed or wholly unmatched; a
partly unmatched selection fails too unless `allow_unmatched_ids = TRUE`, and
even then at least one identifier must match.

```{r direct-ids}
ids <- file.path(docs_root, "demo-patients.csv")
write.csv(data.frame(subject_id = c(10002428, 10000032)), ids, row.names = FALSE)
by_id <- select_cohort_ids(
  "mimic_iv_demo", direct_ids_path = ids, key = "subject_id"
)
by_id
```

SQL is an ordinary query over the source's raw tables. Use `setup_sql` for any
temporary objects the final query needs.

```{r sql-route}
by_sql <- select_cohort_ids(
  "mimic_iv_demo",
  cohort_sql = "SELECT subject_id FROM patients WHERE gender = 'F'",
  key = "subject_id"
)
by_sql
```

An ICD list is a CSV or RDS with `icd_code` and `icd_version` (9 or 10).
`icd_version` is required for MIMIC-IV and eICU and optional for MIMIC-III,
where ICD-9 is assumed; a code matches only in the version it names. The
bundled `scid` cohort is exactly such a list.

## Scope and context

The selected identifier sits somewhere in the source hierarchy: a patient has
admissions, and an admission has ICU stays. `import_scope` says how much of
that hierarchy to keep:

| Scope | Keeps |
|:--|:--|
| `patient` (default) | Every admission and ICU stay of a selected patient |
| `hospital` | Every ICU stay of a selected admission, plus the admission |
| `icu` | Only the selected ICU stay |

An ICD cohort on MIMIC selects hospital admissions, so `icu` cannot narrow it
further; eICU selects ICU stays and accepts all three scopes.

The materialized hierarchy records the decision in three logical columns:
`definition_filter_index` marks the unit the original definition selected,
`scope_index` marks the units kept under the effective scope, and
`patient_context_index` marks everything belonging to the reached patients.
Selecting ICU stay `I1` of patient `P1` with patient scope keeps all of P1's
rows, marks `I1` in `definition_filter_index`, and marks all of them in
`scope_index`. The same selection with ICU scope keeps the same hierarchy but
marks only `I1` in `scope_index`.

The visit windows keep the whole patient context so concepts can look back
across readmissions. Their `in_scope` column is the effective scope, and the
cohort tables carry `cohort_index`, the active membership flag downstream
stages read. Rows with `in_scope = FALSE` are context only: their events were
not imported.

## Materializing

Two destinations implement the same selection, hierarchy and table rules.
Choose Parquet when PostgreSQL is the eventual home:

```{r parquet}
import_cohort_general(
  "mimic_iv_demo",
  cohort = "scid",
  tables = c("patients", "admissions", "icustays"),
  show_progress = FALSE
)
```

The import writes the filtered tables and the window and hierarchy tables next
to the project:

```{r parquet-files}
list.files("cohorts-demo/data/cohorts_data/mimic_iv_demo_parquet")
```

Choose a persistent DuckDB file when DuckDB is the destination:

```{r duckdb}
materialize_cohort_duckdb(
  "mimic_iv_demo",
  db_path = "cohorts-demo/data/duckdb/scid.duckdb",
  cohort = "scid",
  tables = c("patients", "admissions", "icustays"),
  show_progress = FALSE
)
```

Both destinations build the replacement in staging, validate it, and publish it
atomically. A failed build leaves the previous cohort untouched, and the
package keeps no run-version history: one current cohort and one manifest.

The Parquet output contains:

```text
<requested tables>.parquet
admission_windows.parquet
icu_windows.parquet
visit_windows.parquet
cohort_hierarchy.parquet
cohort_meta.parquet
cohort_projection_report.parquet
cohort_table_report.parquet
cohort_icd_evidence.parquet   # ICD routes only
d_<name>_icds.parquet         # ICD routes only
cohort_manifest.json
```

The DuckDB file contains the same tables plus the hierarchy views
`cohort_patient_context`, `cohort_definition_filter` and `cohort_scope`.

## Narrowing later

The three indexes let later code narrow the cohort without losing the original
meaning:

```sql
-- every row of every reached patient
SELECT * FROM cohort_hierarchy WHERE patient_context_index;

-- the units the definition selected
SELECT * FROM cohort_hierarchy WHERE definition_filter_index;

-- the units materialized under the effective scope
SELECT * FROM cohort_hierarchy WHERE scope_index;
```

`cohort_index` on the visit windows and cohort tables is the same membership
flag the pipeline stages read, so filtering on it keeps the cohort definition
intact.

## The Sepsis-3 helper

`build_sepsis_cohort()` wraps the sepsis route when you want the packaged
selection as a file, an import, or both. It defaults to `import_scope =
"hospital"` and offers four variants through `bundle`:

```r
build_sepsis_cohort("mimic_iv_demo", bundle = "first_sepsis3")
```

`sepsis3` keeps every qualifying ICU stay, `first_sepsis3` restricts to the
patient's first-ever ICU stay when it qualifies, `sepsis3_admission` requires
the qualifying suspicion within one day of ICU admission, and
`first_sepsis3_admission` applies both restrictions.
`vignette("sepsis-cohort-definitions")` explains the clinical definitions and
their counts on each source.

## What to remember

- One route per request; the plan is read-only until you materialize.
- `import_scope` controls what is kept; the definition membership is separate.
- Parquet and DuckDB use the same selection engine and the same indexes.
- A replacement is atomic; an interrupted import never destroys the last good
  cohort.
